  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 304 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nToday through Friday night... \n\nStrong surface high pressure across the region will promote quiet \nweather conditions. Current surface analysis early this morning \ndepicts the surface high centered northwest of central Indiana with \nmostly clear skies. Dewpoints in the 50s, light winds, and clear \nskies has allowed for temperatures to cool efficiently overnight \ninto the 50s. Look for additional cooling over the next few hours \nwith lows in the low-mid 50s. A few rural locations across northern \ncounties may fall into the upper 40s. Expect diurnal cumulus to \ndevelop later today once convective temperatures are reached. Deep \nmixing will likely drop dewpoints into the upper 40s to near 50F for \nportions of the area during the afternoon. \n\nExpect passing waves aloft to result in increasing mid-high clouds \ntonight through early Saturday. Most guidance suggest subsidence \ninduced dry air from the surface high should inhibit any \nprecipitation chances so the forecast remains mostly dry. That said, \nsufficient forcing and moisture may be in place across far \nsouthwestern counties to support isolated showers. Look for \ntemperatures to remain cooler than normal with highs generally in \nthe mid 70s to low 80s through the end of the work week.  \n\nSaturday through early next week...\n\nLong range guidance depicts weak surface high pressure remaining the \ndominant weather feature for much of the weekend providing mostly \nquiet weather conditions. Aloft, an amplified ridge centered near \nthe Rockies with northwesterly flow across the Great Lakes Region \nshould allow for a few shortwaves to pass through the region late\nthis weekend into early next week. These disturbances along with \ndeeper moisture advecting into central Indiana will return low \nrain chances to the forecast at times late Sunday into next week.\n\nDiverging model solutions leads to lower confidence in exact details \nduring this period. Widespread severe weather is not expected at \nthis time, but ensemble guidance suggest that at least moderate \ndestabilization will occur each day. This along with enhancement of \nmid-upper level flow from the passing shortwaves could support a \nthreat for severe weather, mainly on Monday. However, this will \ndepend on how far east the upper ridge builds and where the \nshortwaves aloft track. Uncertainty will remain high until models \nbecome better aligned. Latest guidance also depicts an EML \nadvecting towards the region which leads to lower confidence on \nconvective coverage. \n\nWarm air advection ahead of the aforementioned disturbances should \nwarm temperatures well into the 80s to lower 90s Sunday through \nTuesday. Dewpoints returning to the low-mid 70s will likely lead to \npeak heat indices around 100 degrees.",
